% role_policy — what each role in a CoPrompt room is allowed to do

# Module: role_policy

## Intent

A CoPrompt room is not a flat group chat. Each participant joins in a role, and
a team should be able to decide what each role may do: who can start a run, who
can steer one mid-flight, who can halt it, who can edit the shared Intent, and
crucially who gets a vote on whether a proposal becomes a pull request.

Different teams want different shapes. A team that wants QA to hold a hard veto
and everyone else to be advisory should be able to say so. A team that wants a
guest observer who can watch and steer but never vote should be able to say
that too. This module is where that is decided.

Pure decision logic. No I/O, no network, no clock, no global state.

## Interface

```python
POWERS = ("run", "steer", "halt", "edit_intent", "vote", "open_pr")

DEFAULT_POLICY: dict   # role -> {power: bool}, plus "priority" (int)

def resolve(role: str, overrides: dict | None = None) -> dict:
    ...

def can(role: str, power: str, overrides: dict | None = None) -> bool:
    ...

def voters(participants: list[dict], overrides: dict | None = None) -> list[str]:
    ...

def outranks(role_a: str, role_b: str, overrides: dict | None = None) -> bool:
    ...
```

- `role` is one of `"pm"`, `"eng"`, `"design"`, `"qa"`, `"observer"`.
- `overrides` is a partial policy, e.g.
  `{"design": {"vote": False}, "qa": {"priority": 90}}`. It is merged over the
  defaults per role and per power; keys it does not mention keep their default.
- `resolve` returns the full effective entry for one role: every power in
  `POWERS` as a bool, plus an integer `priority`.
- `can` is the single question the server asks before allowing an action.
- `voters` takes `[{"user_id": str, "role": str}, ...]` and returns the
  `user_id`s of those whose role has the `vote` power, sorted ascending. This
  is what gets handed to `approval_quorum` as the member list, so a role
  without a vote is never counted as "waiting on".
- `outranks` compares `priority`; higher wins. Used to decide whose steer the
  agent should weight more when two arrive in the same batch.

## Defaults

| role | run | steer | halt | edit_intent | vote | open_pr | priority |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| pm | yes | yes | yes | yes | yes | yes | 80 |
| eng | yes | yes | yes | yes | yes | yes | 70 |
| design | yes | yes | no | yes | yes | no | 50 |
| qa | yes | yes | yes | no | yes | yes | 60 |
| observer | no | yes | no | no | no | no | 10 |

The default is deliberately permissive for the four working roles: a
collaboration tool that starts by taking powers away from teammates is the
wrong default. `observer` exists so a guest can be given a seat that watches
and suggests without a vote.

## MUST

- MUST return every power in `POWERS` from `resolve`, as a real `bool`, plus an
  integer `priority`.
- MUST merge `overrides` per power, not per role: an override of
  `{"design": {"vote": False}}` MUST leave design's other powers at their
  defaults.
- MUST treat an unknown role as `observer`, so a typo or a future role can
  never silently grant more power than intended.
- MUST return `voters` sorted ascending, and MUST NOT include a duplicate
  `user_id` even if the same id appears twice in `participants`.
- MUST raise `ValueError` from `can` when `power` is not in `POWERS`, so a
  misspelled permission check fails loudly instead of returning `False` and
  silently denying.

## MUST NOT

- MUST NOT mutate `overrides` or `participants`.
- MUST NOT let an override introduce a power name that is not in `POWERS`;
  unknown keys in an override are ignored.
- MUST NOT return `True` for any power of an unknown role except those the
  `observer` default grants.
- MUST NOT perform any I/O.

## Examples

```python
resolve("qa")["vote"]                      # -> True
resolve("observer")["vote"]                # -> False
resolve("nonsense")                        # -> same entry as resolve("observer")

can("design", "halt")                      # -> False
can("design", "halt", {"design": {"halt": True}})   # -> True

# Per-power merge: design keeps steer even though vote was overridden.
p = resolve("design", {"design": {"vote": False}})
p["vote"], p["steer"]                      # -> (False, True)

voters([{"user_id": "amy", "role": "pm"},
        {"user_id": "zed", "role": "observer"},
        {"user_id": "joe", "role": "qa"}])
# -> ["amy", "joe"]                        # zed is not waited on

outranks("pm", "design")                   # -> True
outranks("design", "pm")                   # -> False
outranks("qa", "pm", {"qa": {"priority": 99}})      # -> True

can("pm", "teleport")                      # raises ValueError
```

## Done when

- Every example above returns exactly the stated result.
- The module has no imports beyond the standard library.
